<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cradle of civilization</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cradle of civilization is a place and culture in which one of the earliest civilisations emerged independently, a civilisation being a complex society with a state, social stratification, cities and writing or graphic arts. Scholars once thought civilisation began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> and spread from there; today they generally recognise six cradles: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>–Supe civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. All depended on agriculture, except possibly Caral–Supe. The first large cities, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and Eridu, rose by the 31st century BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest signs of a move to settled life appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> as early as 12,000 BCE, when the Natufian culture became sedentary and went on to become an agricultural society by 10,000 BCE. Fully developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> cultures emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> by 10,200 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> in the Jordan Valley is believed to be the earliest established city, first settled around 9600 BCE. Proto-urban settlements with several thousand inhabitants followed, and by the 31st century BCE Susa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> and Memphis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> joined the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> as places of tens of thousands.</p><p>Scholars once held that civilisation began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> and spread outward by influence. They now think that it arose independently at several places in both hemispheres, and that sedentary and nomadic communities kept interacting rather than standing as separate groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The concept focuses on peoples who built cities, created writing systems, worked pottery and metals, domesticated animals and developed class systems. If civilisation is taken to coincide with writing growing out of proto-writing, the earliest cases are the Near Eastern Chalcolithic of the 4th millennium BCE and proto-writing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a> around 3300 BCE, followed by Chinese writing that developed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oracle-bone-script">oracle bone script</a>, and Mesoamerican systems from about 900 BCE.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> supplied soil and water for irrigation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a>, c. 6500 to 3800 BCE, is the earliest known on the alluvial plain and saw the start of urbanisation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> begins around 3300 BCE with the Early Harappan Phase, which includes the earliest examples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-script">Indus script</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the earliest evidence of cultivated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, at Chengtoushan, dates to 6500 BCE, and the Yangshao culture lasted from 5000 to 3000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>–Supe civilisation on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> emerged around 3200 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruth-shady">Ruth Shady</a> established it as the oldest known civilisation of the Americas in the early 21st century. During the third millennium BCE it may have been the most densely populated area of the world, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> possibly excepted. The roots of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> lie in early farming cultures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> that began around 5100 to 4600 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancient-greece">Ancient Greece</a> and Ancient Rome are called the cradles of Western civilisation because of their part in shaping politics, law, philosophy, art and science. The phrase has also been used to describe the origin of a cultural group or as a basis for national mysticism, as in Indian and Taiwanese nationalism, and it appears in esoteric pseudohistory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cradle of civilization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
